    "content": "# How to Download Instagram Carousels as Photos and Videos\n\nInstagram carousels have transformed how creators and brands share visual stories. Instead of being restricted to a single frame, users can publish up to ten (and recently up to twenty) photos and video clips in a single swipeable gallery post. Carousels are widely used for step-by-step photography guides, educational infographics, event photo dumps, and multi-part video tutorials. However, saving an entire carousel or cherry-picking individual slides through the official Instagram app is virtually impossible without taking low-quality screenshots. In this guide, we show you how to download public Instagram carousels containing both high-resolution photos and Full HD videos on any device.\n\n---\n\n## Table of Contents\n- [What Makes Instagram Carousels Unique?](#what-makes-instagram-carousels-unique)\n- [Why Screenshots Are Not Enough for Carousels](#why-screenshots-are-not-enough-for-carousels)\n- [Step-by-Step: How to Download Instagram Carousels](#step-by-step-how-to-download-instagram-carousels)\n- [Device Guides for iPhone, Android, and Desktop](#device-guides-for-iphone-android-and-desktop)\n- [Troubleshooting Common Carousel Download Issues](#troubleshooting-common-carousel-download-issues)\n- [Managing Mixed Photo and Video Galleries](#managing-mixed-photo-and-video-galleries)\n- [Personal Use, Copyright, and Ethics](#personal-use-copyright-and-ethics)\n- [Related Guides](#related-guides)\n- [Frequently Asked Questions](#frequently-asked-questions)\n\n---\n\n## What Makes Instagram Carousels Unique?\n\nUnlike standalone feed photos or vertical Reels, an Instagram carousel represents a structured array of media items bound to a single post identifier.\n\nA single carousel post can include:\n- Still images in square (1:1), portrait (4:5), or landscape (1.91:1) aspect ratios.\n- High-definition video clips up to 60 seconds each.\n- A seamless panoramic layout where adjacent slides form a continuous image.\n\nWhen viewed inside Instagram, the app pre-loads subsequent slides in the background to ensure fluid swiping. To extract these assets externally, a download tool must parse the underlying JSON metadata manifest and expose direct links to every master file.\n\n---\n\n## Why Screenshots Are Not Enough for Carousels\n\nMany users resort to taking screenshots when they want to save images from a carousel. While convenient, screenshots present significant drawbacks:\n- **Severe Quality Degradation**: Screenshots capture only your screen's pixel resolution, often compressing subtle color gradients and blurring small typography.\n- **Obtrusive UI Elements**: Screenshots capture carousel pagination dots, account tags, like buttons, and status bars that obstruct the original artwork.\n- **Inability to Capture Video Slides**: Screenshots obviously cannot capture dynamic video clips, time-lapses, or audio tracks included in mixed carousels.\n\nUsing the [Instagram Reels downloader](/en/instagram-reels-downloader) on GoSaveClip allows you to retrieve the original, uncropped media files directly from public servers.\n\n---\n\n## Step-by-Step: How to Download Instagram Carousels\n\nFollow these simple steps to download carousel slides:\n\n### Step 1: Copy the Carousel Post Link\nOpen Instagram on your phone or computer. Locate the carousel post you wish to archive. Tap the **three dots** in the upper-right corner (or the paper airplane Share icon) and select **Copy Link**.\n\n### Step 2: Open GoSaveClip Downloader\nOpen your web browser and navigate to the [Instagram Reels downloader](/en/instagram-reels-downloader). Paste the URL into the search box.\n\n### Step 3: Browse and Save Individual Slides\nClick **Download**. The engine inspects the post and displays thumbnails for every image and video slide in the carousel. Click the **Download** button underneath each specific slide you want to save to your device.\n\n---\n\n## Device Guides for iPhone, Android, and Desktop\n\nHere is how to manage downloaded carousel media across different operating systems:\n\n### iPhone and iPad (iOS)\n- Download images and videos in Safari.\n- For photos: tap the image preview, tap **Share**, and select **Save Image** to send it directly to Apple Photos.\n- For videos: tap the video preview, tap **Share**, and tap **Save Video**.\n\n### Android Phones and Tablets\n- Use Google Chrome or Samsung Internet.\n- Files download directly into your device's `Downloads` folder and are immediately accessible in **Google Photos** or your gallery app.\n\n### Desktop Computers (Windows & Mac)\n- Click the download button for each slide.\n- Photos are saved as high-resolution JPEGs, and videos are saved as MP4 files in your `Downloads` directory.\n\n---\n\n## Troubleshooting Common Carousel Download Issues\n\nIf you encounter an issue when saving a carousel, review these four common scenarios:\n\n### 1. \"Only the First Slide Appears in the Downloader\"\n- **Cause**: Some basic downloaders only parse the primary post thumbnail.\n- **Fix**: Use GoSaveClip, which parses the entire carousel slide array and presents individual download buttons for every slide.\n\n### 2. \"Error: Post Belongs to a Private Account\"\n- **Cause**: The creator's profile is set to private.\n- **Fix**: GoSaveClip cannot access private content. The post must be on a publicly accessible profile.\n\n### 3. \"Video Slides Are Missing Audio\"\n- **Cause**: The creator uploaded a silent video slide or muted the audio during publishing.\n- **Fix**: Check the post in Instagram to verify whether the specific video slide has sound.\n\n### 4. \"Browser Opens Image in Tab Instead of Downloading\"\n- **Cause**: Mobile browsers frequently display raw image links inline.\n- **Fix**: Long-press the image on screen and tap **Save to Photos** (on iOS) or **Download Image** (on Android).\n\n---\n\n## Managing Mixed Photo and Video Galleries\n\nMixed carousels containing both photos and videos are increasingly common for product reviews, wedding highlights, and educational demonstrations. When extracting a mixed post on GoSaveClip, our tool automatically identifies the file type of each slide, labeling still photographs as JPEGs and dynamic clips as MP4 videos so you always download the appropriate format.\n\n---\n\n## Personal Use, Copyright, and Ethics\n\nGoSaveClip is intended exclusively for personal, non-commercial, and educational reference. All photographs, graphics, and video presentations belong to their original creators. Downloading content you do not own for unauthorized reposting, commercial advertising, or digital redistribution can violate copyright laws. When citing or sharing excerpts of a creator's carousel, always credit their Instagram handle prominently and seek their explicit consent beforehand.\n\n---\n\n## Related Guides\n\nCheck out our other guides for mastering Instagram media:\n\n- Read our guide on how to [save Instagram Reels with audio to camera roll](/en/blog/save-instagram-reels-with-audio-to-camera-roll).\n- Learn how to [save Instagram Reels on Android without an app](/en/blog/save-instagram-reels-android-without-app).\n- Discover how to [extract audio from an Instagram Reel as MP3](/en/blog/instagram-reel-audio-to-mp3).\n\n---\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n### Can an Instagram carousel contain both photos and videos in the same post?\nYes. Instagram allows creators to mix still photos and video clips within a single carousel post. GoSaveClip parses the entire media array so you can download each asset in its native format.\n\n### Do I have to download every slide in a carousel at once?\nNo. GoSaveClip breaks down the carousel into individual slides, allowing you to selectively download only the photos or videos you want to keep.\n\n### Are carousel videos downloaded with their original audio?\nYes. Any video slide in a public carousel is downloaded as an MP4 file with its original synchronized audio intact.\n\n### Can I download carousels from private Instagram profiles?\nNo. GoSaveClip strictly accesses publicly visible media. Carousel posts from private accounts cannot be extracted.",
